### (c) Number prefix and digit case

- **RECOMMENDATION:**
  - **Base prefixes:** lowercase `0x`, `0b`, `0o` **only** (`0X`, `0B`, `0O` refused).
  - **Hexadecimal digits:** lowercase `0`..`9` and `a`..`f` **only** (`A`..`F` refused).
- **ALTERNATIVE:**
  - Lowercase base prefixes `0x`, `0b`, `0o` only, but allow both lowercase `a`..`f` and uppercase `A`..`F` hex digits (as drafted in [docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:102](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md#L102)).
- **REASONS:**
  - **One form per situation ([docs/PHILOSOPHY.md:54-57](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/PHILOSOPHY.md#L54-L57) Rule 2):** Allowing `0x` and `0X`, or `0xff` and `0xFF` and `0xFf`, creates multiple surface spellings for the exact same hexadecimal token and leaves work for the formatter at slice 17 to canonicalize.
  - **Item 5 wording ([OWNER_29_DECISIONS_2026-11-08.md:15-16](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/OWNER_29_DECISIONS_2026-11-08.md#L15-L16)):** Item 5 explicitly names `200u8` and "the prefixes 0x, 0b and 0o" in lowercase.
  - **Visual boundary with suffixes:** With integer type suffixes in lowercase (`u8`, `i64`, etc.), lowercase hex digits (`0xffu8`) keep a uniform lowercase token alphabet without mixed-case noise, and since `u` and `i` are disjoint from `a`..`f`, the boundary between hex digits and suffix is unambiguous in a single forward scan.
- **CONFLICTS WITH DECIDED ROWS:**
  - No conflict with any decided row in [docs/decisions.md](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/decisions.md).
  - *Note on legacy spec examples:* Two prose examples in the legacy spec chapters use uppercase hex digits (`0x1F` in [legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:473](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md#L473) SYN-6 and `0xFF` in [legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md:47](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md#L47), [72](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md#L72) ARITH-2), and [docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:102](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md#L102) wrote `hex_digit = 0…9 | a…f | A…F`. Those legacy examples are non-decided prose and migrate to `0x1f` / `0xff` when encoded in Lean.

