### (d) The suffix grammar and one-form reconciliation with OD31 / OD138

- **RECOMMENDATION:**
  1. **Exact suffix set:** The 8 canonical integer type names only:
     ```ebnf
     INT_SUFFIX = "i8" | "i16" | "i32" | "i64" | "u8" | "u16" | "u32" | "u64" ;
     ```
     **No float suffixes** (`f32`, `f64` are refused on float and integer literals).
  2. **Allowed on every base, with lexical boundary refusal:**
     ```ebnf
     INT = (DECIMAL | HEX | BINARY | OCTAL), [INT_SUFFIX] ;
     ```
     Permitted on all four bases (e.g., `200u8`, `0xffu8`, `0b1010u8`, `0o77u8`). In the lexer, after reading the base prefix and digits (or after `FLOAT` digits), any immediately following `[A-Za-z0-9_]+` tail is consumed as part of the numeric token candidate: if it is not in `INT_SUFFIX` (for `INT`) or is non-empty (for `FLOAT`), the lexer refuses the whole token (`123u9`, `0xffg`, `123_456`, `1.5f64`) as a single malformed numeric literal rather than splitting it into a number followed by an identifier.
  3. **Reconciling with OD31, OD138, and the one-form rule (Rule 2):**
     - **Option (1) — Best long-term one-form rule (context-Complementary):** An integer literal is **unsuffixed** wherever OD138 supplies an expected integer type from context (an annotated `let`, a function parameter, a `return`, a struct field in a typed literal, or the other operand of a binary operator when that operand already has a known type, [docs/decisions.md:1428](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/decisions.md#L1428)), and **requires a suffix** only where OD138 supplies no expected type (such as `let x = 200u8;`, an untyped generic struct constructor `S { a: 200u8 }` as in Q6.2, or a literal-to-literal comparison `3u8 < 4`). Writing a redundant suffix where context already fixes the type (`let x: u8 = 200u8;`) is refused as a duplicate type specification.
     - *Note on Q6.2:* If `S` in Q6.2's `let S { a } = S { a: 200u8 };` ([docs/decisions.md:191](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/decisions.md#L191)) is a non-generic struct `struct S { a: u8 }`, its field `a` is a declared position under OD138. If `S { a: 200u8 }` is meant to be legal even on a non-generic struct with a `u8` field, see the Alternative below.
- **ALTERNATIVE:**
  - **Permissive optional suffixes:** Allow `INT_SUFFIX` optionally on any integer literal in any position (checked for exact type equality with the expected type when one exists, and supplying the type when no expected type exists), plus allow `f32` / `f64` suffixes on `FLOAT` literals for symmetry.
- **REASONS:**
  - **One form per situation ([docs/PHILOSOPHY.md:54-57](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/PHILOSOPHY.md#L54-L57) Rule 2, [68-74](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/PHILOSOPHY.md#L68-L74) Rule 5):** Under unrestricted optional suffixes, `let x: u8 = 200;` and `let x: u8 = 200u8;` are two ways to write the same binding, and `let x: u8 = 200u8;` restates the type fact `u8` twice in four tokens. Making suffixes and context-typing complementary (unsuffixed when context determines the type per OD31/OD138; suffixed when context does not) ensures there is never more than one way to write a literal at any site.
  - **Why float suffixes are omitted:** OD63 ([docs/decisions.md:1353](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/decisions.md#L1353)) decided that float literals take their expected type and are refused where none exists, and Item 5 ([OWNER_29_DECISIONS_2026-11-08.md:14-20](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/OWNER_29_DECISIONS_2026-11-08.md#L14-L20)) and Q6.2 ([docs/decisions.md:191](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/decisions.md#L191)) only introduce integer suffixes (`200u8`, `123u9`).
  - **Base orthogonality ([docs/PHILOSOPHY.md:208-209](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/PHILOSOPHY.md#L208-L209) AT 4):** Allowing `INT_SUFFIX` across all four bases (`DECIMAL`, `HEX`, `BINARY`, `OCTAL`) avoids an "except when" clause in the lexer and is well-defined because lowercase hex digits `a`..`f` do not overlap the suffix starters `i` and `u`.
  - **Maximal-munch refusal of malformed suffixes ([OWNER_29_DECISIONS_2026-11-08.md:20](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/OWNER_29_DECISIONS_2026-11-08.md#L20)):** Consuming any trailing `[A-Za-z0-9_]+` after a number and validating it against `INT_SUFFIX` directly enforces Item 5's rule that `123u9` (and `123_456`) is refused as a malformed number token rather than split into `123` and `u9`.
- **CONFLICTS WITH DECIDED ROWS:**
  1. **ARITH-2@4 rationale ([legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md:47-53](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md#L47-L53)):** Explicitly states *"suffixes such as `7u8` are not part of the language (an annotation or a conversion says the type)"*. Item 5 ([OWNER_29_DECISIONS_2026-11-08.md:14-15](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/OWNER_29_DECISIONS_2026-11-08.md#L14-L15)) revises and supersedes that statement in ARITH-2's rationale.
  2. **Slice-0 surface ([docs/decisions.md:445](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/decisions.md#L445), [docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:20](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md#L20), [386](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md#L386)):** Slice 0 requires *"explicitly typed immutable `u8` bindings, representable decimal literals"* (`let x: u8 = 20;`) and puts all integer widths/bases at slice 2 ([docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:22](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md#L22)). In slice 0, every `let` is annotated (`let x: u8 = ...`), so unsuffixed decimal literals remain the slice-0 form, and typed integer suffixes arrive at slice 2 (or are refused as "not supported in this slice" in slice 0 per [docs/decisions.md:897-899](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/decisions.md#L897-L899)).
  3. **Tension between Rule 2 (one form), OD31/OD138 (context typing + field as a declared position), and Q6.2 / Item 5 (`S { a: 200u8 }`):** Under OD138 ([docs/decisions.md:1428](file:///tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/round-lexspell/src/docs/decisions.md#L1428)), a struct field is already a declared position that supplies an expected type (`S { a: 200 }`), unless `S` is generic (`struct S<T> { a: T }`, where OD46 infers `T` from the argument `200u8`). If `200u8` is also permitted in positions that already have an expected type (`let x: u8 = 200u8;` or non-generic `S { a: 200u8 }`), that is an explicit owner exception to Rule 2 (two ways to write a typed literal in context-typed positions) and should be recorded as such.

